{"usCongressBioId":"H000841","familyName":"Howard","givenName":"John","middleName":"Eager","unaccentedFamilyName":"Howard","unaccentedGivenName":"John","unaccentedMiddleName":"Eager","birthDate":"1752-06-04","birthCirca":false,"birthDateUnknown":false,"deathDate":"1827-10-12","deathCirca":false,"image":[{"contentUrl":"/bioguide-staging/assets/53cb44ec2613e85bd19858ca58ecfed4.jpg","caption":"<a href=\"http://www.senate.gov/artandhistory/history/common/generic/Photo_Collection_of_the_Senate_Historical_Office.htm\">Courtesy U.S. Senate Historical Office</a>","name":"H000841_001"}],"profileText":"a Delegate and a Senator from Maryland; born in Baltimore County, Md., June 4, 1752; was instructed by private tutors; served in the the Revolutionary War, captain and then holding the rank of colonel when peace was declared; was voted a silver medal and the thanks of Congress for gallantry at the Battle of Cowpens 1781; Delegate to the Confederation Congress, 1788; Governor of Maryland, 1789-1791; member of the Maryland state senate, 1791-1795; elected as a Federalist in 1796 to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of Richard Potts; reelected on December 9, 1796 and served from November 21, 1796, to March 3, 1803; served as President pro tempore of the Senate during the Sixth Congress; offered the position of Secretary of War by President George Washington, but declined; also declined a commission as brigadier general in the expected war with France in 1798; unsuccessful Federalist candidate for Vice President in 1816; died on October 12, 1827, at 'Belvedere,' near Baltimore, Md.; interment in Old St. Paul's Cemetery, Baltimore, Md.","relationship":[{"relatedTo":{"usCongressBioId":"H000835","familyName":"Howard","givenName":"Benjamin","middleName":"Chew","honorificPrefix":"Mr.","unaccentedFamilyName":"Howard","unaccentedGivenName":"Benjamin","unaccentedMiddleName":"Chew","birthDate":"1791-11-05","birthCirca":false,"birthDateUnknown":false,"dea